[[Governor of Missouri|Governor]] [[Mike Parson]] took office on June 1, 2018, upon the resignation of [[Eric Greitens]] and was elected to a full term in his own right in [[2020 Missouri gubernatorial election|2020]] with 57.2% of the vote. Because Parson served more than two years of Greitens' term, he was [[Term limits in the United States|term-limited]] by the [[Missouri Constitution]] in 2024 and could not seek re-election for a second full term. Incumbent [[List of lieutenant governors of Missouri|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Mike Kehoe]] won the Republican gubernatorial nomination with 39% of the vote, defeating [[Missouri State Senate|state Senator]] [[Bill Eigel]] and [[Missouri Secretary of State|Secretary of State]] [[Jay Ashcroft]].<ref name=Kehoe>{{Cite web |last=Hancock |first=Jason|date=March 22, 2021 |title=Mike Kehoe passes on 2022 Senate run to focus on 2024 Missouri governor's race|url=https://missouriindependent.com/briefs/mike-kehoe-passes-on-senate-run-in-2022-to-run-for-missouri-governor-in-2024/|access-date=September 7, 2021 |website=Missouri Independent}}</ref> In the Democratic primary, [[Missouri House of Representatives|state House]] [[Minority Leader]] [[Crystal Quade]] won the nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-07-09 |title=Democrat Crystal Quade shows off roller derby skills in Missouri governor campaign launch video |url=https://apnews.com/article/missouri-election-governor-crystal-quade-roller-derby-077fb887f8893abba85fe804fb074ffc |access-date=2023-07-18 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>{{Election box begin no change|title=Republican primary results<ref name=NYTresults>{{cite web |title=Missouri Governor Primary Election Results 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=August 6, 2024 |access-date=August 7, 2024 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/08/06/us/elections/results-missouri-governor-primary.html}}</ref>}}
