Governor [[Doug Burgum]] was re-elected to a second term in [[2020 North Dakota gubernatorial election|2020]] with 65.8% of the vote. In the November 2022 elections, voters amended the [[North Dakota Constitution]] to place a limit of two, four-year terms for succeeding governors sworn into office after the amendment's effective date of January 1, 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |last=MacPherson|first=James |date=November 8, 2022 |title=North Dakota voters OK term limits for governor, legislators |url=https://www.stltoday.com/news/national/govt-and-politics/north-dakota-voters-ok-term-limits-for-governor-legislators/article_fc775b59-4c32-5054-aa6c-933faeb8c075.html |url-status=dead |access-date=November 20, 2022 |newspaper=St. Louis Post-Dispatch |agency=Associated Press | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221110015413/https://www.stltoday.com/news/national/govt-and-politics/north-dakota-voters-ok-term-limits-for-governor-legislators/article_fc775b59-4c32-5054-aa6c-933faeb8c075.html | archive-date=November 10, 2022}}</ref> Burgum, sworn into office before the amendment's effective date, remained eligible to run for re-election for a third term. Burgum initially ran for the [[2024 Republican Party presidential primaries|Republican nomination]] in the [[2024 United States presidential election]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-05-26 |title=A North Dakota governor running for president? You betcha |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/north-dakota-gov-doug-burgum-considers-presidential-bid-rcna85307 |access-date=2023-05-27 |last=Gomez |first=Henry J.|website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> but dropped out before the primaries. On January 22, 2024, he announced he would not seek re-election as Governor. [[United States House of Representatives|United States Representative]] [[Kelly Armstrong]] won the Republican nomination and defeated [[Lieutenant Governor of North Dakota|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Tammy Miller (politician)|Tammy Miller]].<ref name=Burgum/>

On the other side, [[North Dakota State Senate|state Senator]] [[Merrill Piepkorn]] won the Democratic nomination unopposed.{{Election box begin no change|title=Republican primary results<ref>{{cite web | url=https://apnews.com/projects/election-results-2024/north-dakota/?r=35004# | title=2024 North Dakota Election Results | website=[[Associated Press News]] }}</ref>}}
