[[Governor of Puerto Rico|Governor]] [[Pedro Pierluisi]] was elected in [[2020 Puerto Rico gubernatorial election|2020]] with 32.9% of the vote. He announced his re-election campaign on March 20, 2022, during the [[New Progressive Party (Puerto Rico)|New Progressive Party]]'s general assembly.<ref name="calienta">{{Cite web |title=Pierluisi calienta motores para las elecciones del 2024 |url=https://www.noticel.com/politica/ahora/top-stories/20220320/pierluisi-calienta-motores-para-las-elecciones-del-2024/ |access-date=2022-10-19 |website=Noticel |language=es-US}}</ref> However, he was defeated by [[Jenniffer González-Colón]], the [[Republican Party (US)|Republican]] affiliated [[Resident commissioner of Puerto Rico|resident commissioner]], in the New Progressive Party primary election.<ref name=PierluisiConcedes/>

Territorial legislator [[Jesús Manuel Ortiz]] won the nomination of the [[Popular Democratic Party (Puerto Rico)|Popular Democratic Party]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Puerto Rico Rep. Jesús Manuel Ortiz wins gubernatorial primary |date=June 2, 2024 |access-date=July 7, 2024 |work=[[Voice of America]] |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/puerto-rico-rep-jes%C3%BAs-manuel-ortiz-wins-gubernatorial-primary/7640173.html}}</ref> Other candidates include former territorial senator [[Juan Dalmau]] of the [[Alianza de País]] (an alliance between the [[Puerto Rican Independence Party]] and [[Movimiento Victoria Ciudadana|Citizen's Victory Movement]]), and [[San Sebastián, Puerto Rico|San Sebastián]] mayor [[Javier Jiménez (mayor)|Javier Jiménez]] of [[Proyecto Dignidad|Project Dignity]]. Because Citizen's Victory was required by law to field a candidate in the governor's race, Javier Córdova Iturregui was also on the ballot. Ultimately, González won the election.
